nations with  this we'll do a forward pass  calculate the loss and then if that loss  is less than the previously lowest  seen loss we'll update our best weights  and biases along with updating our  lowest seen loss  if we run this we will indeed see that  the loss did decline  some but only just barely value-wise  and accuracy does not improve at all so  what could we do instead  we see that randomly searching for  weight and bias combinations  is probably going to take far too long  again the problem that we're trying to  solve here  is an extremely simple problem this  should be nearly instantly solvable  so randomly searching for weight and  bias combinations  is just going to be unacceptable on any  decent  problem maybe rather than randomly  searching for totally new combinations  every single time  we could instead make random tweaks to  the  current best known weights and biases  all we have to change to do this is  change the assignment  to instead being a plus equals  if this adjustment decreases loss then  we'll keep it and we'll continue to try  adjustments from this new point  otherwise the weights and biases stay  exactly where they were before  now when we run with this concept we can  see that we've actually come up with an  okay solution for this problem  we see continued progress for quite a  while and we end with about 0.17 loss  in 93 accuracy so again all we did here  was rather than just randomly picking  combinations of weights and biases  we just randomly tweaked from the last  best known  combination of weights and biases for  the lowest loss value  so have we found a simpler way of doing  optimization  of neural networks is calculus not  required at all  well remember that we're using that more  basic  vertical data set and that this data set  could be easily solved with just some  perfectly vertical lines and this would  probably indeed get you  93 about percent accuracy so this is  really just a data set that we  don't even need deep learning at all to  solve so now let's change from the  vertical data set back to the  spiral data set this data set still has  clear and obvious classes but  is not solvable by just some vertical  lines we need  a non-linear solution here  with the semi-random logical-ish  tweaking from the best weights and  biases code that we used before  we can run this and see that the outcome  while it does learn  is still a loss that's greater than 1.0  and an accuracy that floats around  40 percent it's of course still possible  that we could continue tweaking the  logic that we're using here for  semi-random optimization but  even this spiral data set is actually a  very basic data set for neural networks  and the complexity of a model required  to learn this would be a pretty small  model  but the problem with randomly searching  is the search space  even if it's a very small model the  search space is still theoretically  infinite so this is still a huge problem  with random search  another issue here is that we're  treating each of the weights and biases  identically when it's likely that  certain weights and certain biases  might be more influential and impactful  than others  also in the end it's likely we reached  what's known as a local minimum  this is a concept we'll talk more on  later but definitely going to continue  being a problem we would have to add  some further logic to help us get out of  this  it turns out that hard problems are hard  to solve and this is why we turn to  calculus which can help us to  intelligently navigate to a solution the  first step towards this navigation  is understanding the impact of a  variable on some function  in our case that's going to be the  impact of the weights and the biases  on the loss per each sample since  samples too can impact the model  differently while the loss function  is a function that we apply to a model's  output not the weights and biases the  weights and biases are directly  influencing that model's output  so it's really the impact of the weights  and biases  on the loss function that we're  interested in  now let's start with a simple function  to understand what we mean by  impact this function takes an input x  and multiplies it by two now let's add  some code to visualize this and we'll  create some inputs  x using python's range and then y will  be the output we can also graph this to  get an idea for what it looks like  so with f of x equals 2x or y  equals 2x what's the impact of x  on y in this case it should be fairly  obvious  it's 2 y will always be double whatever  x  is another way to describe this impact  in the case of a linear function like  this  is the slope which is defined as the  change in y  over the change in x or delta y over  delta x  with a linear function we can calculate  the slope from any two points  let's say x equals zero and x equals one  so that means point one would be two x  or two times zero so y would be zero  for point two that would also be two x  or two times one  so y would be two the delta for y in  this case  is from zero to two so that's two the  delta for x  is from zero to one so it's one so the  slope is two divided by one  meaning the slope is 2.  in python we can calculate this by  indexing y at index  1 minus y at the index of 0 dividing  that  by x at the index of 1 minus x at the  index  of zero giving us again the same answer  the slope is two  but what happens when we have a  non-linear function like  two x squared which makes a graph like  this  depending on which two points that we  use the calculation for the slope  is going to vary so how do we measure  the  impact that x has on y in the case of a  non-linear function like this  calculus proposes that we use the slope  of the tangent line  the tangent line is the quote-unquote  instantaneous slope  between two points that are  quote-unquote infinitely close  on a curve this instantaneous slope  is also the derivative and it should be  noted that this can only be done  on a continuous curve so no sharp  corners or anything like that at least  at the moment we have no idea how to get  these two  infinitely close points on a curve but  we could approximate this by simply  adding a  very small delta from some sample point  let's imagine that we'll use a delta of  0.0001  this means that 0.1 would be 1 and .2  will be one plus that tiny delta now  we'll calculate the slope between these  two very close points which gives us an  approximate derivative  of about four we'll soon learn that the  actual derivative for two x squared at  x equals one is indeed exactly 4. the  small difference here  is because we're using an approximation  technique this is what's known as  numerical differentiation  and it produces the numerical derivative  it's a solution where we just calculate  the derivative  which is the tangent line's slope by  using two points that are infinitely or  at least in our case sufficiently  close the further away the two points  are for this calculation on a curve  the larger the inaccuracy is likely to  be so this is why we want to use two  extremely close points  to help with visualizing this in python  we'll modify our code slightly so that  our graph is created with much smaller  steps at a time creating a smoother  curve finally for us to calculate this  line we need one more element  the y-intercept or the b in the equation  for a line which is y equals  mx plus b the slope of a line is handled  in the mx part  and for now we're just saying the slope  m is 1  for example but we also need to know how  to calculate the b variable if we want  to visualize things  since the equation for a line is y  equals mx plus b  if we already know y and m but we want  to solve for b  we can use basic algebra to say b equals  y  minus mx or using our code's variable  names  b equals y2 minus the approximate  derivative  times x2 so the derivative is the slope  applying this to our existing code we  can now calculate and draw a section of  that tangent line  which you can see as a green line on the  graph on the right  taking this a step further we can add a  list of colors move up the approximate  tangent line  function and add the approximate  derivative and b  as parameters then we'll create a loop  of five iterations  with a for i in range five tabbing over  the calculating and graphing code  we'll then replace x1 to be whatever i  is  then we're going to add a scatter so we  can mark each point that we'll be doing  this calculation of numerical  differentiation with a dot  colored uniquely for that specific point  and then finally we'll be modifying our  plot function to  update with the new parameters for that  approximate tangent line function  and we'll be adding colors  we can then run this calculating the  tangent lines and the slopes  of those tangent lines for each point  along the way using numerical  differentiation  so again what's the point of all this in  order to  intelligently know how to adjust  parameters in a function to make some  desired change  we need to be able to have some way of  calculating the impact of those  parameters on the output of the function  now a slight issue for us is that the  actual function  in a neural network is not so simple as  having a single variable  oftentimes we're attempting to adjust  millions or more  parameters calculating derivatives using  numerical differentiation  would require multiple forward passes  for a single weight  or bias adjustment per sample  this is because for each sample  we'll do a forward pass calculating the  loss at the end  then for each weight and bias  individually  one at a time we'll add that small delta  do another forward pass  calculating the loss again then we'd  revert that parameter back to the  original  state choose a new one add the delta  repeat the forward pass  and continue this for every single  weight and bias  in the network note that here we're only  displaying the deltas  and passes for each weight but this  would also be required for each  individual bias too  and again we need to be doing this  process not only for every single weight  and bias  individually but we need to be doing all  of that  per each sample this would still most  likely be a  faster and more successful approach than  doing things randomly  but we can still do a little better  we're definitely on the right track with  derivatives but using numerical  derivatives in this way would be similar  to brute forcing the problem or  something like that  it would work but it would be slower  than ideal  for multivariate functions instead of  doing something like multiple forward  passes for our neural network  we can use partial derivatives which are  indeed coming up but first we need to  cover the analytical derivative which is  the subject of the next tutorial  as you may be aware if you are enjoying  the content and want another medium to  absorb the material with you can check  out the neural networks from scratchbook  which is fully complete for making  optimizing and testing both  classification and regression neural  networks  you can get the ebook hardcover or  softcover at nnfs.io  we've now sold over 10 000 copies at the  time of this recording which is  pretty staggering thank you to all of  you who have chosen to spend your time  and or money on learning this content  and i will see you all in the next video  you

